framework SuccessorDeliveryPlan:
  requires problem and decision
  requires premise and evidence
  warns pending

domain CharacterSemanticMigrationSuccessorPlanV1:
  description "Sequence post-requirement work without conflating the urgent authoring defect, architecture authority, local implementation, paid provider use, or production mutation."

problem P1:
  |
    Requirement revision 6 is accepted, but it deliberately leaves the
    successor architecture, implementation, provider-backed candidate creation,
    production migration, and readback unapproved. Production also has a
    separate response-schema identity defect that prevents new character
    creation, while eight current ready V2 characters remain unselectable.

evidence E_REQUIREMENT:
  |
    docs/character-v2-compatibility-requirements-v6.md R1-R25 selects a hybrid
    LLM-assisted V2-to-V3 semantic migration, forbids permanent V2 reader
    compatibility, preserves immutable generations and battle bindings, and
    separates requirement acceptance, ADR acceptance, implementation,
    deployment, authoring-policy activation, provider execution, production
    pointer migration, and rollback.

evidence E_ACCEPTANCE:
  |
    docs/character-v2-compatibility-requirements-v6-acceptance.md records exact
    owner acceptance and explicitly retains gaps for successor ADRs, V3 schema,
    capsule, consumer-scoped compatibility, repair and receipt contracts,
    implementation, paid provider work, per-asset acceptance, production
    migration, and readback.

evidence E_CONFLICT:
  |
    Proposed ADR-0029 revision 1 selects a permanent V2 compatibility reader.
    The accepted requirement says it must not be accepted as written and must be
    revised or replaced before implementation of the semantic migration.

evidence E_PRODUCT_DEFECT:
  |
    The current worktree contains a focused response-schema identity correction
    and regression test in backend/src/llm/openai-compatible.ts and
    backend/src/llm/openai-compatible-character-definition.test.ts. The accepted
    requirement classifies this as a necessary but separate authoring defect and
    requires its output contract to follow the selected schema after cutover.

evidence E_EXISTING_PLAN:
  |
    docs/dialogue-expression-realization.pert revision 19 still treats t029 as
    active and then schedules the conscious-agency comparison. It does not yet
    represent the newly accepted semantic-migration architecture, implementation,
    migration rehearsal, owner candidate acceptance, or production restoration.

evidence E_SEAL:
  |
    Sealgraph impact from acceptance/character-semantic-migration-v6 currently
    reports no downstream Seals and stale reports none. This means the new
    successor plan must explicitly link its accepted-requirement cause; zero
    stale does not prove that older plans already account for the new requirement.

premise AUTHORITY:
  |
    Planning may identify later gates, but it does not itself accept an ADR,
    authorize provider calls, deploy code, activate schema 3, mutate production
    generations or pointers, or accept migrated candidates.

premise DELIVERY:
  |
    The shortest safe path restores new-character authoring independently, then
    fixes the architecture contradiction before semantic-migration code. Runtime
    readers for historical battle bindings must exist before schema-3 cutover,
    and production candidate generation must wait for deployable dual-version
    code, dry-run evidence, and a frozen manifest.

premise OPTIONS:
  |
    A combine authoring repair, architecture, migration engine, provider calls,
    and production pointer changes in one task. B serialize every concern even
    when independent, delaying the authoring repair. C use two coordinated lanes:
    finish and verify the independent authoring defect first while the migration
    lane proceeds through ADR, contracts, local implementation, rehearsal, and
    separately authorized production gates.

pending U1:
  |
    The exact successor ADR decomposition, qualified V3 compiler and provenance
    names, capsule persistence and retention, retry ceilings, semantic-review
    implementation, authoring-policy storage, provider budget, deployment route,
    frozen production manifest, and per-asset candidate outcomes remain unknown
    until their designated tasks and reviews.

decision D1 based_on P1, E_REQUIREMENT, E_ACCEPTANCE, E_PRODUCT_DEFECT, AUTHORITY, DELIVERY, OPTIONS:
  |
    Select option C. First close the already-started response-schema correction
    with focused and full local verification, without calling a provider or
    claiming live recovery. In parallel planning terms, but not by unauthorized
    external execution, start the migration lane with a successor ADR package.

decision D2 based_on D1, E_CONFLICT, E_REQUIREMENT, E_ACCEPTANCE, U1:
  |
    Make the first migration-lane deliverable a proposed successor architecture
    that withdraws Proposed ADR-0029 revision 1 and refines ADR-0010 and ADR-0011.
    It must close the schema, capability, capsule, preservation, change-set,
    semantic-review and bounded-repair, receipt and idempotency, authoring-policy,
    migration commit, and rollback choices before implementation. Exact owner
    acceptance remains a separate gate.

decision D3 based_on D2, E_REQUIREMENT, DELIVERY, U1:
  |
    After ADR acceptance, implement in four locally verifiable slices: shared V3
    contracts and compiler boundaries; durable attempt, request, capsule, and
    candidate persistence; structural plus semantic repair orchestration and
    owner-review diff; append-only per-asset activation, replay, drift, and
    rollback controls. Each slice adds focused tests and Seal links and ends with
    full typecheck, tests, build, duplication, and Lizard verification before the
    next effect boundary.

decision D4 based_on D3, E_REQUIREMENT, AUTHORITY, DELIVERY, U1:
  |
    After local implementation, deploy and verify dual V2/V3 read compatibility
    before selecting schema 3. Then run a no-provider, no-write dry-run over a
    freshly frozen and owner-reviewed eight-generation manifest. Provider-backed
    candidate generation, owner acceptance of exact per-asset digests, append and
    compare-and-swap pointer activation, schema-3 authoring-policy activation, and
    production readback are distinct, sequentially approved tasks with stop and
    rollback gates.

decision D5 based_on D1, D2, D3, D4, E_EXISTING_PLAN, E_SEAL:
  |
    Record a dedicated successor plan rather than rewriting completed conscious-
    agency history. Update the active PERT and backlog only to add explicit tasks
    and dependencies, keep t029 completion review and t030-t032 agency quality
    comparison distinct, link the new plan to the accepted requirement Seal, and
    review and reseal every impacted planning artifact. The next executable task
    after plan approval is the local response-schema repair closeout; the next
    architectural task is the proposed successor ADR package.
